  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to represent the step function as a vectorized composition of basis functions, transforming the high-dimensional optimization into a lower-dimensional space. This approach reduces the dimensionality while preserving the essential properties of the function. We'll use a combination of:\n- Initial basis functions adapted from the parent solution\n- A trust-region optimization strategy\n- Bi-level optimization between representation parameters and sample points\nThis differs from the previous code by using explicit vectorization and basis function optimization instead of grid-based refinement.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\na. Representation: h(x) = Normalized\u2211weights_i * basis_i(x)\n   - Basis functions: B-splines of degree 3\n   - Normalization ensures integral=1 and range[0,1]\nb. Variables:\n   - bspline_knots: Reduced number (70 knots)\n   - Control points: Initial from parent solution mapped to basis\n   - Smoothing parameter: \u03bb \u2208 [0.1, 1.0] to balance sparsity and smoothness\nc. Objective: Exploitable overlap = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   - The full integral is approximated via Simpson's rule with 2000 points\n   - Implemented as `correlate_with_kernel` for efficient computation\nd. Constraints:\n   - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 ensured by basis vector clipping\n   - Integral constraint built into basis construction\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Basis setup:\n   - Initialize B-spline basis (degree=3, 70 knots)\n   - Map parent solution to control points using curve-fitting\nb. Trust-region optimization:\n   - Iterative steps with \u03c4 \u2208 [0.01, 0.5] system radius\n   - Use `scipy.optimize.minimize` with COBYLA and SR1\n   - Smoothness enforcement via Tikhonov regularization\nc. Bi-level updates:\n   - Start with initial control points from parent solution\n   - Apply 50 chunks of updates:\n     - Chunk 1-10: Basis smoothing\n     - Chunk 11-30: Control point refinement\n     - Chunk 31-50: Strided evaluation\n   - Every 10 chunks: Save checkpoint\nd. Feasibility checks:\n   - Project to feasible space using L-embedded projection\n   - Check boundaries whenever regularization fails\ne. Return strategy:\n   - Best solution in terms of overlap reduction\n   - Checkpoint validation every 5 chunks\n   - Final evaluation with exact parent metric\n\n4. Search choices\na. Initial parameters:\n   - \u03c40 = 0.1 (initial trust region)\n   - Max iterations: 2000\n   - Feasibility projection probability: 0.7\nb. Adaptive strategy:\n   - When stuck: Double grid resolution\n   - When overlap improves: Reduce \u03c4 multiplier\n   - When time < 300s: Jump to exponential schedule\n\n5. Compute budget\na. Estimation:\n   - Basis construction: O(2000) operations\n   - Each optimization chunk: O(50) projected gradients\n   - Total: 700,000 evaluations\nb. Time allocation:\n   - 20% time: Basis refinement\n   - 50% time: Control point optimization\n   - 30% time: Bi-level updates\nc. Sanity checks:\n   - Monitor NaN/Inf in metrics\n   - Gracefully degrade if memory exhausted\n\n6. Validation and return\na. Checks:\n   - Integral: |sum(h)*dx - 1| < 1e-7\n   - Bounds: Check all h(x) \u2208 [0.001, 0.999]\n   - Overlap evaluation: Match parent's compute_c5 method\nb. Return format:\n   - Projected control points to grid every 25 chunks\n   - Save every 0.5% improvement\n   - Final output: (h_values, best_score, n_points)\nc. Fallback:\n   - If time < 5% completes: Return initial solution\n   - If dimension too high: Reduce knots to 30\n\nThe dimensionality reduction via B-splines allows more aggressive exploration while maintaining mathematical soundness. The trust-region approach balances exploration and exploitation, while the bi-level updates enable adaptability to complex optimization landscapes.",
